---
short_label: III.24
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Similar segments of circles on equal straight lines equal one another.
----
proof:

Let {AEB} and {CFD} be similar segments of circles on equal straight lines {AB} and {CD}.

I say that the segment {AEB} equals the segment {CFD}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="350" height="110" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="III.24 diagram" src="propIII24.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // A superposition proof, like I.4 and I.8. Joyce's figure draws the two
    // segments as arcs (BEA, DFC), their chords, and the third curve CGD —
    // the "cuts across" case — as two arcs (GC, DG) meeting at G on the arc
    // CFD. Everything added here is fully transparent (;0;0;0 lines,
    // ;0;0;0;0 circles / polygons), so the resting figure is unchanged.
    //
    // The superposition itself is A.Polygon.superpose on a transparent
    // triangle A,B,E laid onto C,D,F' — F' is the figure's own `similar`
    // point, the image of E under the motion that carries A to C and AB to
    // CD, and it lies on the arc CFD. The ghost is a gold outline of chord +
    // apex; the arcs themselves never move.
    //
    // CGD is one curvedTriangle (#119) over the two arc carriers and the
    // chord, so the caption's {CGD} lights the whole curve at once.
    var GIVEN = ["A","B","AB","E","BEA","C","Ccirc","D","CD","F'","CF'G","F","DFC"];
    var SUPER = GIVEN.concat(["AEBt","CFDt"]);
    var CUT   = SUPER.concat(["G","GC","DG","Hcirc","Kcirc","CGD"]);
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "III.24",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;40,80",
            "B;point;free;160,80",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "E;point;free;90,30;black;0",
            "BEA;sector;arc;B,E,A;0;0;black;0",
            "C;point;free;190,80",
            "Ccirc;circle;radius;C,AB;0;0;0;0",
            "D;point;circleSlider;Ccirc,310,80",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "F';point;similar;C,D,A,B,E;0;0",
            "CF'G;circle;circumcircle;D,F',C;0;0;0;0",
            "F;point;circleSlider;CF'G,230,35;black;0",
            "DFC;sector;arc;D,F,C;0;0;black;0",
            "G;point;circleSlider;CF'G,270,40",
            "GCH;polygon;equilateralTriangle;G,C;0;0;0;0",
            "H;point;vertex;GCH,3;0;0",
            "GC;sector;sector;H,G,C;0;0;black;0",
            "M;point;midpoint;D,G;0;0",
            "M';point;perpendicular;M,D;0;0",
            "K;point;intersection;M,M',H,G;0;0",
            "DG;sector;sector;K,D,G;0;0;black;0",
            // Superposition carriers: chord + apex of each segment. Vertex 0
            // lands on vertex 0 (A on C) and side 0-1 on side 0-1 (AB on CD).
            "AEBt;polygon;triangle;A,B,E;0;0;0;0",
            "CFDt;polygon;triangle;C,D,F';0;0;0;0",
            // The cutting curve CGD as one hover / highlight target: its two
            // arcs lie on the circles about H and K, its third side is CD.
            "Hcirc;circle;radius;H,G;0;0;0;0",
            "Kcirc;circle;radius;K,G;0;0;0;0",
            "CGD;polygon;curvedTriangle;C,G,D,Hcirc,Kcirc,CD;0;0;0;0"
        ],
        // G is a circleSlider, and draggables are auto-unioned into every
        // slide — it would stand lettered on the canvas from slide 1, before
        // the proof reaches "as CGD". Deferring drops it out of that union.
        // A, B, E, C, D, F are draggable too, but all are givens of slide 1.
        // The resting figure is unaffected.
        deferDraggables: ["G"],
        aliases: { "AEB": "BEA", "CFD": "DFC", "BA": "AB", "DC": "CD" },
        slides: [
            // The deck opens on the proposition's statement over the givens
            // (process.md “Open on the statement”).
            { text: "Similar segments of circles on equal straight lines equal one another.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: [],
              justifications: [{ ref: "III.24" }] },
            { text: "Let {AEB} and {CFD} be similar segments of circles on equal straight lines {AB} and {CD}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["BEA","DFC","AB","CD"] },
            { text: "I say that the segment {AEB} equals the segment {CFD}.",
              visible: GIVEN,
              highlighted: ["BEA","DFC"] },
            { text: "For, if the segment {AEB} is superposed on {CFD}, and if the point {A} is placed on {C} and the straight line {AB} on {CD}, then the point {B} coincides with the point {D}, because {AB} equals {CD},",
              visible: SUPER,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD"],
              transition: { animations: [
                  { elem: "AEBt", name: geomlib.A.Polygon.superpose,
                    args: { onto: "CFDt" } } ] } },
            { text: "and, {AB} coinciding with {CD}, the segment {AEB} also coincides with {CFD}.",
              visible: SUPER,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD","BEA","DFC"] },
            { text: "For, if the straight line {AB} coincides with {CD} but the segment {AEB} does not coincide with {CFD}, then it either falls within it, or outside it, or it cuts across it, as {CGD},",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["DFC", "GC", "G", "DG"],
              transition: { mode: "cascade", animations: [
                  { elem: "GC", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep },
                  { elem: "G",  name: geomlib.A.Point.appear },
                  { elem: "DG", name: geomlib.A.Sector.sweep } ] },
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "neither within it nor outside it", ref: "III.23" } ] },
            { text: "and a circle cuts a circle at more points than two, which is impossible.",
              visible: CUT,
              highlighted: ["DFC","CGD"],
              justifications: [
                  { claim: "a circle cuts a circle at more points than two", ref: "III.10" } ] },
            // The cutting curve was the impossible case; it is dropped here.
            { text: "Therefore, if the straight line {AB} is superposed on {CD}, then the segment {AEB} does not fail to coincide with {CFD} also, therefore it coincides with it and equals it.",
              visible: SUPER,
              highlighted: ["AB","CD","BEA","DFC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "C.N.4" }] },
            { text: "Therefore similar segments of circles on equal straight lines equal one another.",
              visible: SUPER,
              highlighted: ["BEA","DFC"],
              justifications: [{ ref: "Q.E.D." }] }
        ],
        resolveJustification: function(ref) {
            if (ref === "III.24") return "#prop";
            return (window.eucrefs && window.eucrefs[ref]) || null;
        }
    });

    // Per-page citation lookup for the slideshow's resolveJustification.
    window.eucrefs = (window.eucrefs || {});
    Object.assign(window.eucrefs, {
        "III.10": "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII10/",
        "III.23": "/elements/books/bookIII/propositions/propIII23/",
        "C.N.4":  "/elements/books/bookI/commonnotions/cn4/"
    });
</script>
</figure>

For, if the segment {AEB} is superposed on {CFD}, and if the point {A} is placed on {C} and the straight line {AB} on {CD}, then the point {B} coincides with the point {D}, because {AB} equals {CD}, and, {AB} coinciding with {CD}, the segment {AEB} also coincides with {CFD}.

For, if the straight line {AB} coincides with {CD} but the segment {AEB} does not coincide with {CFD}, then it either falls within it, or outside it, or it cuts across it, as {CGD}, and a circle cuts a circle at more points than two, which is impossible. [!just III.23; III.10]

Therefore, if the straight line {AB} is superposed on {CD}, then the segment {AEB} does not fail to coincide with {CFD} also, therefore it coincides with it and equals it. [!just C.N.4]

Therefore *similar segments of circles on equal straight lines equal one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

The proof here uses the method of superposition which was also used for @I.4 and @I.8.

This proposition is used in @III.26.
